</tbody>
</table>

The successful case stays at the expected URL and shows an approved order with its confirmation. With the button missing, the click rejects and the order remains visibly `Pending`. Unexpected navigation is easier to miss because the click resolves just as it does on success, even though the required order result is absent.

## Define approval from the task input

For order `1001`, approval requires the expected URL and three exact values: the requested order ID, the status `Approved`, and the matching confirmation. Each set of result markers must contain exactly one element, and that element must be visible and have the expected text. This ties the decision to the requested order, so approval words elsewhere on the page aren't enough.

~~~javascript
function oneVisibleValue(values, expected) {
  return values.length === 1 &&
    values[0].visible && values[0].text === expected;
}

function isCompleted(observed, expectedURL, input) {
  return observed.url === expectedURL &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.order, input.orderId) &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.status, 'Approved') &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.confirmation,
      `Order ${input.orderId} approved`);
}
~~~

`isCompleted` reads neither the fake's report nor the fixture mode. It also rejects duplicate markers, even when their text matches, because the task requires a single order result.

## Keep the click outcome separate

The fake passes `Approve order` to Taiko's [button selector](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/button/) and attempts a [click](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/click/). It records whether the call resolved, but returns `completed` in both branches:

~~~javascript
async function optimisticAgent() {
  try {
    await taiko.click(taiko.button('Approve order'),
      { navigationTimeout: 30000 });
    return { report: 'completed', actionResolved: true, error: null };
  } catch (error) {
    return {
      report: 'completed', actionResolved: false,
      error: {
        name: error.name, message: error.message, stack: error.stack },
    };
  }
}
~~~

You can see the mismatch in `unexpected-navigation.json`: the report is `completed` and `actionResolved` is `true`, while the final URL ends in `/session-expired`. The approval check returns `false` because the expected order result is missing. In the missing-button case, the rejected click helps explain why the order is still pending.

That missing-button case expects an `Error` with this exact message, including two ordinary spaces before `not`:

~~~text
Button with label Approve order  not found
~~~

The complete runner is `workflow.test.cjs` in the [`taiko-agent-task` folder](https://github.com/tridev-tej/technical-blog-examples/tree/005c62af00ce3cac768dc6e1e5d551fcc246859a/taiko-agent-task) of `technical-blog-examples`. Its `runScenario` function checks the starting page before attempting the action, then reads the page again for `isCompleted`. The file also contains `readPage` and the case assertions.

## Read the page without changing the result

The observer reads the URL with [currentURL](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/currenturl/), the DOM through [evaluate](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/evaluate/), and the number of approval buttons with [elements](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/elementwrapper/). Its callback reads the page without adding markers or changing the status:

~~~javascript
async function observe() {
  const url = await taiko.currentURL();
  const dom = await taiko.evaluate(readPage,
    { navigationTimeout: 30000 });
  const buttons = (
    await taiko.button('Approve order').elements(0, 0)).length;
  return { url, ...dom, buttons };
}
~~~

`isCompleted` uses the result markers. The observer also saves the body text for a weaker check that accepts any occurrence of `Order 1001 approved`. The expired page has that phrase but none of the markers.

Visibility here is approximate. The check uses box dimensions, intersection with the viewport, and ancestor styles; it doesn't establish that a marker is unobscured or accessible.

The URL, DOM, and button count are sequential readings, so they aren't an atomic snapshot: the page can change between them. If your application updates asynchronously, you also need to decide when its state has settled enough to read.

## Check the misleading phrase

A phrase search accepts the expired page from the opening:

~~~javascript
function textOnlyCompleted(observed, input) {
  return observed.bodyText.includes(`Order ${input.orderId} approved`);
}
~~~

To expose that false positive, the control visits `/session-expired` directly and asserts that `textOnlyCompleted` returns `false`. The function returns `true`, so the assertion raises `AssertionError` with `true !== false`. The control passes only when it captures that expected error; no error or a different error name makes it fail. A passing control records the phrase search's false positive; the full predicate rejects the same page.

The verdict concerns this fixture's DOM state, which the success handler changes synchronously. Checking persisted approval would also require evidence from the storing system. Authentication, payments, production workflows, frames, shadow DOM, delayed updates, and broken-connection recovery are outside this example.

## Run the example

The runner requires Linux and uses Node's built-in test runner. The recorded setup used Linux x64, Node 22.23.1, npm 10.9.8, Taiko 1.5.0, chrome-remote-interface 0.33.3, and freshly installed bundled Chrome 147.0.7727.117. Both recorded runs passed the same four cases, with no failures or skips. Other platforms and versions are untested.

Use the pinned `technical-blog-examples` revision below and run the example from `taiko-agent-task`. Keep all five reader files together, including the unchanged `package-lock.json` beside `package.json`.

Normal `npm ci` install scripts download the browser, so installation may use the network. Before running the commands, unset every `TAIKO_*` variable, plus `LOCAL_PROTOCOL`, `NODE_OPTIONS`, and `NODE_PATH`: the reader rejects them. The XDG config and cache directories must be new and writable.

Run these commands in order from a directory where you want to clone the repository:

~~~sh
git clone https://github.com/tridev-tej/technical-blog-examples.git
cd technical-blog-examples
git checkout --detach 005c62af00ce3cac768dc6e1e5d551fcc246859a
cd taiko-agent-task
node --version
npm --version
npm ci
npm ls taiko --depth=0
mkdir xdg-config xdg-cache
XDG_CONFIG_HOME="$PWD/xdg-config" \
  XDG_CACHE_HOME="$PWD/xdg-cache" npm test
XDG_CONFIG_HOME="$PWD/xdg-config" \
  XDG_CACHE_HOME="$PWD/xdg-cache" npm test
~~~

Each `npm test` run creates an `evidence-*` directory. Start with `unexpected-navigation.json` and compare it with `success.json`: both clicks resolved, but only one page has the required order result. The final-page PNGs let you inspect what the browser showed, and `text-only-control.json` records the expected failure of the phrase search.

## Choose evidence for your next task

Choose the expected URL and result values from the new task's input, then decide when the page is settled enough to read. Give the check a page like `/session-expired` as a counterexample: it has the success words without the required result. The check should still reject that page when the click resolves and the agent reports `completed`.
